From 518924622a6c298958f49f309a98ebe29699a0fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 01:37:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [meta] warn on calls to functions without specification in
 reading/writing contexts

A call to an undefined function is checked through the callee's assigns
(writing) or \from (reading) clauses. A behavior without assigns gives the
warning "Cannot analyze footprint of function f: no assigns clause", and an
assigns clause without \from gives "Cannot fully analyze read footprint of
function f: no \from part in some assigns clauses", both without call-site
location. A function declared without any specification has no behavior at
all, so the call was skipped silently. This is the case of every libc
function when the code is parsed with the system headers
(-no-frama-c-stdlib, the default of e-acsl-gcc): memcpy(&protected, ...)
gets no instance and no message.

Emit the same warning when a callee has no behavior, put all three
missing-footprint warnings under a new warning category no-footprint so
they can be silenced or turned into errors consistently, and pass the
call-site statement location (~loc) so each warning reports the file and
line of the call.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
